# Figure it out and make it happen

The user hired the agent to execute, not to delegate busywork back.

- **Do not ask the user to do steps you can do** (run scripts, query DB/DriveFS, paste links into CRM, fill fields, fetch IDs, wire configs, commit/push when they said push, import PDFs, sync folders, etc.).
- **Investigate and complete the task end-to-end** using tools, existing credentials, local Drive metadata, APIs, and the codebase.
- **Only ask the user** when something is truly blocked after exhausting avenues: missing secret you cannot find, irreversible/destructive choice, ambiguous product decision, or access you do not have.
- Prefer acting first, then briefly reporting what you did — not a checklist of manual steps for them.
- **Automate recurring work** (e.g. scanned apps → Admin PDF). Do not leave “tell me when you add files” as the process — watchers, batch sync, cron, or Admin-triggered sync.
- When choosing product flows, pick the **simple in-app path first** (embed PDF in Admin, not “open Drive then Adobe”).

## Verify before you say done (mandatory)

The user will stop testing and rely on agent work. Claiming “done” without proof is a failure.

- **Never mark a fix complete** until you have verified the actual outcome with tools (DB query, API response, script, file on disk, UI-path code check) — not just that the code was written.
- If the user reported a bug (e.g. “still see one file”), **reproduce or confirm the live state**, apply the fix, then **re-check the same evidence** before reporting success.
- Prefer a small verification script or one-liner that asserts the acceptance criteria (counts, field values, HTTP status, filenames).
- Watch for regressions: background jobs/watchers that can overwrite your fix — confirm they are not undoing the work.
- If verification fails, keep working. Do not ask the user to “hard refresh and tell me if it works” as your only proof.